Right — two sources, one resolved config. This is the standard pattern: Prettier, ESLint, Vitest, and Vite itself all do it. Let me design the merge strategy properly.

---

## The four-layer resolution stack

```
Defaults
  ↓ overridden by
Auto-detection (react version, monorepo, panda, etc.)
  ↓ overridden by
docgen.config.ts (project/workspace level)
  ↓ overridden by
Inline plugin options (instance level — highest precedence)
  ↓
ResolvedOptions
```

The inline options win, which means a monorepo can share a `docgen.config.ts` at the workspace root while individual Storybook instances override specific fields:

```ts
// docgen.config.ts (workspace root — shared)
export default defineConfig({
  presets: [presets.shadcn(), presets.reactAria()],
  propFilter: (prop) => !prop.parent?.fileName.includes('node_modules'),
  cache: './node_modules/.cache/oxc-react-docgen',
})

// packages/marketing-site/.storybook/main.ts (per-instance override)
viteFinal: (config) => ({
  ...config,
  plugins: [...config.plugins, oxcReactDocgen({
    include: ['../src'],      // overrides auto-detected srcDirs
    react: '18',              // this package still on React 18
  })]
})
```

---

## Merge semantics per field type

Not all fields should use "last wins". Arrays are additive:

```ts
type MergeStrategy = 'override' | 'concat' | 'deep-merge'

const FIELD_STRATEGY: Record<keyof Options, MergeStrategy> = {
  // Scalars — inline wins outright
  react: 'override',
  crossPackage: 'override',
  cache: 'override',

  // Arrays — concat across layers (presets from config file + presets inline)
  presets: 'concat',
  plugins: 'concat',
  variantFunctions: 'concat',
  exclude: 'concat',

  // Arrays — override (explicit include should replace, not add)
  include: 'override',

  // Objects — deep merge (notableProps.button from config + notableProps.input inline)
  notableProps: 'deep-merge',
  paths: 'deep-merge',
  knownTypeOverrides: 'deep-merge',

  // Functions — compose (inline propFilter AND config propFilter must both pass)
  propFilter: 'compose',
}
```

The `propFilter` composition is important — if both layers define a propFilter, a prop must pass both:

```ts
function mergeOptions(...layers: Partial<Options>[]): ResolvedOptions {
  let propFilters: PropFilter[] = []

  for (const layer of layers) {
    if (layer.propFilter) propFilters.push(layer.propFilter)
    // ... other fields per strategy
  }

  return {
    // ...merged fields...
    propFilter: propFilters.length === 0
      ? defaultPropFilter
      : (prop, comp) => propFilters.every(f => f(prop, comp)),
  }
}
```

---

## Config file discovery — walk up, stop at workspace root

```ts
async function findConfigFile(startDir: string): Promise<string | null> {
  let dir = startDir
  const root = await findWorkspaceRoot(startDir)  // stops at pnpm-workspace.yaml etc.

  while (true) {
    const candidate = path.join(dir, 'docgen.config.ts')
    if (fs.existsSync(candidate)) return candidate

    if (dir === root) return null  // reached workspace root, not found
    const parent = path.dirname(dir)
    if (parent === dir) return null  // filesystem root
    dir = parent
  }
}
```

This means a config file at the workspace root is automatically inherited by all packages — no explicit setup needed. Same model as `tsconfig.json` path walking.

---

## `extends` for explicit composition

```ts
// packages/components/docgen.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  extends: '../../docgen.config.ts',  // explicit parent
  include: ['./src'],                  // adds to parent's include (if any)
  react: '19',                         // overrides parent
})
```

Resolves as: load parent → merge child → result. No circular extends allowed (check and throw a clear error). Depth limit of 5 to catch accidental cycles. This is the same design as `tsconfig.json`'s `extends` and ESLint's `extends`.

---

## What this means for the CLI

The CLI always uses the same resolution stack — it just doesn't have an "inline plugin options" layer. Config file + auto-detection + CLI flags:

```bash
# Uses docgen.config.ts automatically
oxc-react-docgen extract

# Explicit config file location
oxc-react-docgen extract --config ./tooling/docgen.config.ts

# CLI flags are the "inline" layer for the CLI
oxc-react-docgen extract --src ./src --react 19 --no-cross-package
```

The `--src`, `--react`, `--no-cross-package` flags map to the same `ResolvedOptions` fields as inline plugin options. One resolution stack, three entry points (Vite plugin, Rolldown plugin, CLI).

---

## The `ResolvedOptions` type is the contract

Everything collapses into one type that both the TS wrapper and the Rust NAPI layer understand:

```ts
// This is what crosses the NAPI boundary — always fully resolved, no optionals
interface ResolvedOptions {
  srcDirs: string[]
  exclude: string[]
  react: '18' | '19'
  crossPackage: boolean
  cache: string | false
  variantFunctions: string[]
  pandaOutdir: string | false
  vanillaExtract: boolean
  knownTypeOverrides: Record<string, KnownTypeOverride>
  tsconfigPath: string | null
  extraPaths: Record<string, string[]>
}

// These stay in TS — can't cross the NAPI boundary as functions
interface TsSideOptions {
  propFilter: (prop: PropItem, component: ComponentEntry) => boolean
  plugins: OxcDocgenPlugin[]
  notableProps: Partial<Record<HtmlElement, string[]>>
}
```

The split is clean: everything that can be serialized to JSON goes to Rust. Everything that's a function stays in TypeScript and runs as a post-processing step after NAPI returns.

---

## Concretely, the plugin init flow

```ts
export function oxcReactDocgen(inlineOptions: Options = {}): Plugin[] {
  let resolved: ResolvedOptions
  let tsSide: TsSideOptions
  let sessionId: number

  return [
    {
      name: 'oxc-react-docgen:resolve',
      enforce: 'pre',

      async configResolved(config) {
        const detected = await autoDetect(config.root)
        const configFile = await loadConfigFile(config.root)  // walks up to workspace root

        // Four-layer merge
        const merged = mergeOptions(DEFAULTS, detected, configFile, inlineOptions)

        resolved = merged.rust    // serializable → goes to NAPI
        tsSide = merged.ts        // functions → stays in TS

        const api = await getNapi()
        sessionId = api.createSession(resolved)
      },

      async transform(code, id) {
        if (!filter(id)) return null
        await extractionReady

        let component = extractionResult[id]
        if (!component) return null

        // Apply TS-side transforms — propFilter, plugins
        component = applyTsSideTransforms(component, tsSide)
        if (!component) return null  // filtered out entirely

        return { code: `${code}\n${buildDocgenBlock(component)}`, map: null }
      },
    },
    virtualModulePlugin(() => ({ resolved, tsSide, extractionResult })),
    diagnosticsPlugin(() => sessionId),
  ]
}
```

This is clean, testable, and has no surprises. The user experience is smooth regardless of whether they use a config file, inline options, or both — it all flows through the same stack.

