## Default Value Architecture

There are three sources, with clear precedence:

```
1. Code (destructuring)  ← always wins, most accurate
2. @default / @defaultValue in JSDoc/TSDoc  ← used when no code default
3. defaultProps static property  ← legacy, still common in MUI .d.ts files
```

### What the extractor needs to do

Currently `ComponentMapping` captures the component name and props type name but loses the parameter destructuring entirely. We need to add:

```rust
pub struct ComponentMapping {
    // ...existing fields...
    /// Default values extracted directly from parameter destructuring.
    /// Key = prop name, value = evaluated default.
    /// Populated only for .tsx/.ts implementation files, not .d.ts.
    pub param_defaults: FxHashMap<String, RawDefault>,
}

pub struct RawDefault {
    pub value: String,   // the literal value or source expression
    pub computed: bool,  // true if we couldn't statically evaluate it
    pub source: DefaultSource,
}

pub enum DefaultSource {
    Destructuring,  // const Button = ({ size = 'md' }: Props) => ...
    DefaultProps,   // Button.defaultProps = { size: 'md' }
    JsDoc,          // /** @default 'md' */
}
```

### Extracting from destructuring

When the extractor finds a component with a params pattern, it traverses the destructuring:

```rust
fn extract_param_defaults<'a>(&self, params: &FormalParameters<'a>) -> FxHashMap<String, RawDefault> {
    let mut defaults = FxHashMap::default();

    let Some(first_param) = params.items.first() else { return defaults };
    let BindingPatternKind::ObjectPattern(obj) = &first_param.pattern.kind else { return defaults };

    for prop in &obj.properties {
        let name = self.binding_prop_name(&prop.key);
        let Some(default_expr) = prop.value.default.as_ref() else { continue };
        defaults.insert(name, self.eval_expr_as_default(default_expr));
    }
    defaults
}

fn eval_expr_as_default<'a>(&self, expr: &Expression<'a>) -> RawDefault {
    match expr {
        Expression::StringLiteral(s) =>
            RawDefault { value: format!("\"{}\"", s.value), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring },
        Expression::NumericLiteral(n) =>
            RawDefault { value: n.value.to_string(), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring },
        Expression::BooleanLiteral(b) =>
            RawDefault { value: b.value.to_string(), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring },
        Expression::NullLiteral(_) =>
            RawDefault { value: "null".into(), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring },
        Expression::Identifier(id) if id.name.as_str() == "undefined" =>
            RawDefault { value: "undefined".into(), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring },

        // Array/object literals — extract the raw source span, not computed
        Expression::ArrayExpression(_) | Expression::ObjectExpression(_) => {
            let raw = self.span_text(expr.span());
            RawDefault { value: raw.into(), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring }
        }

        // Identifier that might be a const in this file — extract raw, mark computed
        // Resolver can optionally resolve if it's a same-file const literal
        Expression::Identifier(id) => {
            RawDefault { value: id.name.as_str().into(), computed: true, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring }
        }

        // Everything else: template literals, calls, ternary — extract source text
        _ => {
            let raw = self.span_text(expr.span());
            RawDefault { value: raw.into(), computed: true, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring }
        }
    }
}
```

### The resolver merge — where code vs JSDoc conflict detection lives

```rust
fn resolve_default(
    prop_name: &str,
    raw_prop: &RawProp,
    mapping: &ComponentMapping,
    diagnostics: &mut Vec<Diagnostic>,
) -> Option<DefaultValue> {
    let code_default = mapping.param_defaults.get(prop_name);

    // @default (JSDoc) or @defaultValue (TSDoc) — both recognized
    let jsdoc_default = raw_prop.tags.get("default")
        .or_else(|| raw_prop.tags.get("defaultValue"))
        .map(|s| s.trim());

    match (code_default, jsdoc_default) {
        // Code and JSDoc both present — code wins, warn if they differ
        (Some(code), Some(jsdoc)) => {
            let code_val = code.value.trim_matches('"');
            if code_val != jsdoc {
                diagnostics.push(Diagnostic {
                    severity: DiagnosticSeverity::Info,  // Info not Warning — not a bug, just staleness
                    message: format!(
                        "prop '{}': @default \"{}\" in JSDoc differs from code default {} — code value used",
                        prop_name, jsdoc, code.value
                    ),
                    help: Some("Update the @default annotation to match the implementation".into()),
                    code: DiagnosticCode::JsDocDefaultMismatch,
                    ..
                });
            }
            Some(DefaultValue { value: code.value.clone(), computed: code.computed })
        }

        // Code only
        (Some(code), None) =>
            Some(DefaultValue { value: code.value.clone(), computed: code.computed }),

        // JSDoc only — common for .d.ts files where there's no implementation
        (None, Some(jsdoc)) =>
            Some(DefaultValue { value: jsdoc.to_owned(), computed: false }),

        (None, None) => None,
    }
}
```

### For `.d.ts` files — JSDoc is the only source

Library `.d.ts` files have no implementation code. JSDoc `@default` is authoritative there and should be trusted without a diagnostic:

```ts
// @types/react/index.d.ts (conceptually):
interface ButtonHTMLAttributes {
    /** @default 'button' */
    type?: 'button' | 'submit' | 'reset'
}
```

Since `.d.ts` files never produce `param_defaults` (no function bodies), the resolver naturally falls through to the JSDoc-only branch — correct by design.

### `defaultProps` scanning (MUI, legacy React)

```rust
// In the extractor — scan for ComponentName.defaultProps = { ... }
fn visit_assignment_expression(&mut self, node: &AssignmentExpression<'a>) {
    if let Expression::MemberExpression(mem) = &node.left {
        if mem.property_name() == Some("defaultProps") {
            if let Some(comp_name) = self.member_object_name(mem) {
                if let Expression::ObjectExpression(obj) = &node.right {
                    let defaults = self.extract_object_defaults(obj);
                    // Store against the component mapping for comp_name
                    self.pending_default_props.insert(comp_name, defaults);
                }
            }
        }

        // Also catch: ComponentName.displayName = "Name"
        if mem.property_name() == Some("displayName") {
            // ...existing displayName handling...
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Addressing the Identified Issues

### ✅ JSDoc description inheritance order

The research flagged that react-docgen gets this wrong — parent description overrides child. In our resolver, own props are appended after parent props and use `BTreeMap` insertion which overwrites on duplicate keys. This is correct IF own props come last. Need to verify Phase 3a agent implements it this way and add a specific test:

```rust
// Test case to add:
// Interface Child extends Parent { /** Child desc */ onClick: ... }
// Interface Parent { /** Parent desc */ onClick: ... }  
// Result should have description = "Child desc"
```

### ✅ Callable component detection (rare but worth handling)

```ts
// Pattern: interface that has a call signature returning JSX
interface SelectComponent {
    (props: SelectProps): JSX.Element       // ← call signature = this IS the component
    Option: typeof SelectOption             // ← static compound component
    displayName?: string
}
```

Add to the extractor — when visiting `TSInterfaceDeclaration`, check if any body member is a `TSCallSignatureDeclaration` returning `JSX.Element` or `ReactElement`. If so, emit a `ComponentMapping` using the interface name.

### ✅ Const reference resolution for defaults

```tsx
const DEFAULT_VARIANT = 'default' as const
function Button({ variant = DEFAULT_VARIANT }: ButtonProps) {}
```

The extractor already scans `as const` objects for enums. Extend it to also index top-level `const X = <literal>` declarations into a `same_file_consts: FxHashMap<String, String>` map. Then in `eval_expr_as_default`, when we see an `Identifier`, check this map:

```rust
Expression::Identifier(id) => {
    if let Some(val) = self.same_file_consts.get(id.name.as_str()) {
        RawDefault { value: val.clone(), computed: false, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring }
    } else {
        RawDefault { value: id.name.as_str().into(), computed: true, source: DefaultSource::Destructuring }
    }
}
```

This turns `DEFAULT_VARIANT` → `"default"` at extract time, not resolver time.

### ⚠️ Intersection types in prop definitions (partially solved)

The issue `Type1 | Type2 & Type3` failing in react-docgen is a parser bug. OXC parses this correctly — we get the right AST. The open question is what the resolver does with a prop typed as an intersection:

```ts
interface ButtonProps {
    style?: CSSProperties & { elevation?: number }  // intersection in prop type
}
```

Currently we store this as a raw string `"CSSProperties & { elevation?: number }"`. The resolver sees a raw type string and tries to parse it as a `Named` type — which fails on the `&`. 

The fix: in `classify_type_alias`, add intersection types in raw prop types to the `Intersection` variant, and the resolver expands each member separately. This is a Phase 3a resolver task to add to the spec.

### ⚠️ Complex computed defaults — emit helpful diagnostic

```tsx
function Button({ id = generateId() }: ButtonProps) {}
```

We extract `value: "generateId()"`, `computed: true`. The diagnostic we emit in the Storybook output should say something useful:

```
defaultValue: { value: "generateId()", computed: true }
```

Storybook renders `computed` defaults in italics with a note. This is better than what RDT does (silently drops it).

### ❌ Conditional/mapped types — degrade with excellent diagnostics

OXC confirmed it will never resolve these. Our `Opaque { reason: ConditionalType }` is the right permanent answer. But the diagnostic message should be genuinely helpful:

```
Cannot resolve conditional type: T extends string ? 'a' : 'b'
  help: Conditional types require the TypeScript compiler to evaluate.
        Consider extracting this to a concrete type alias, or document it 
        manually using /** @default 'a' */
        For advanced use, set resolveConditionalTypes: true to use typescript-go (slower).
```

That last option — `resolveConditionalTypes: true` as an escape hatch that spawns `typescript-go` — is worth adding to `PipelineOptions` as a future flag. Don't implement now but stub the option so the config shape is forward-compatible.

---

## New `DiagnosticCode` variants needed

```rust
pub enum DiagnosticCode {
    // ...existing...
    JsDocDefaultMismatch,      // @default conflicts with code default
    ComputedDefault,           // default is a runtime expression
    CallableComponent,         // component detected via call signature interface
    ConditionalTypeSkipped,    // conditional/mapped type degraded to Opaque
}
```

---

## Summary of changes before Phase 3

**`types.rs`** — add `RawDefault`, `DefaultSource` structs, `param_defaults` field on `ComponentMapping`, new `DiagnosticCode` variants

**Phase 2a follow-up** (small agent, parallel with Phase 3) — update extractor with:
- `extract_param_defaults()` from destructuring
- `eval_expr_as_default()` with const resolution
- `defaultProps` assignment scanning
- Callable interface component detection
- `same_file_consts` index

**Phase 3a resolver** — default merge logic with discrepancy detection, intersection type expansion in prop types, JSDoc description inheritance order test

Shall I update `types.rs` and `docs/08-OPEN-QUESTIONS.md` with all of this now, then launch Phase 3?

