STEP 1 — Add a pre-parse nesting-depth guard. In `parse_file` (mod.rs), before calling `Parser::new(&allocator, source, source_type).parse()`, add a cheap linear scan of the raw source text counting maximum nesting depth of `(`, `{`, `[` (single running counter incremented on any open bracket, decremented on any close bracket; track the max value seen — you only need to bound depth, not verify balance):
```rust
const MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH: usize = 2000;

fn max_bracket_nesting_depth(source: &str) -> usize {
    let mut depth: i64 = 0;
    let mut max_depth: usize = 0;
    for b in source.bytes() {
        match b {
            b'(' | b'{' | b'[' => {
                depth += 1;
                if depth as usize > max_depth {
                    max_depth = depth as usize;
                }
            }
            b')' | b'}' | b']' => depth -= 1,
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    max_depth
}
```
If the observed depth exceeds MAX_SOURCE_NESTING_DEPTH, skip parsing entirely and return a SourceData with a single Diagnostic (see Step 2 for the code) instead of calling Parser::parse(). Build the SourceData via SourceDataCollector::new + finish(), or SourceData::default() if simpler — read the existing struct definitions to see what's idiomatic here.

STEP 2 — Add DiagnosticCode::ExcessiveNesting and DiagnosticCode::ParseError to the enum in crates/core/src/types/diagnostic.rs, following the exact existing pattern for derives/serde attributes already on that enum (look at existing variants like IoError, UnresolvableImport for the pattern). If a `rust-types` skill is available, use it to check your new public enum variants against this project's checklist.

STEP 3 — Surface OXC's own parse errors as diagnostics. In parse_file, after `let ret = Parser::new(&allocator, source, source_type).parse();`, check `ret.errors` (Vec<OxcDiagnostic> from the oxc_diagnostics crate) — for each one, push a Diagnostic{severity: Error, code: DiagnosticCode::ParseError, message: <the OxcDiagnostic's message, use its Display/to_string>, file: path.clone(), line: None, column: None, help: None} onto the collector's diagnostics before `collector.visit_program(&ret.program)`. OXC's parser is error-recovering so ret.program is still usable — parse anyway, just also report the errors. line/column: None is acceptable (deriving them from byte spans is a separate, lower-priority follow-up, not required here).

