You're investigating a real bug in oxc-react-docgen (a Rust workspace at the repo root — a fast TypeScript/TSX React-prop extractor built on OXC). This is a research spike — figure out whether the bug is tractable to fix without a full type checker, and if so, sketch the approach. You are NOT expected to ship a production fix in this pass, but if the fix turns out small and low-risk, feel free to implement it with TDD. Nothing here should be committed if you don't finish a clean fix — the worktree you're in will be discarded if you make no changes, or can be handed back as a diff.

## The bug

A newly-added fixture at `fixtures/ark-ui/types.ts` and the original (since-rewritten) form of `fixtures/ark-ui/Select.tsx` used this pattern (adapted from real `chakra-ui/ark`, a real design system):

```ts
type Assign<T, U> = Omit<T, keyof U> & U;

interface SelectRootBaseProps<T extends CollectionItem> {
  collection: ListCollection<T>;
  multiple?: boolean;
  disabled?: boolean;
  // ...
}

type SelectRootProps<T extends CollectionItem> = Assign<HTMLProps<'div'>, SelectRootBaseProps<T>>;

// Exported as:
const SelectRoot = forwardRef(SelectImpl) as SelectRootComponent; // where SelectImpl: <T,>(props: SelectRootProps<T>, ref) => ...
```

When the resolver tries to resolve `SelectRootProps<T>` for a concrete component instantiation, it needs to walk into the user-defined generic type alias `Assign<T, U>`, substitute the actual type arguments (`HTMLProps<'div'>` for `T`, `SelectRootBaseProps<T>` for `U`), and then resolve the substituted body (`Omit<T, keyof U> & U`). Currently this doesn't happen — the resolver treats `T`/`U` as literal unresolved type names rather than substituting the real arguments, and the props come back completely empty.

Note this is DIFFERENT from the "generic parameter substitution" gap documented in `docs/type-checker-integration.md` as deferred/hard — that doc is about React's OWN builtin generics (e.g. `DOMAttributes<T>`, `*EventHandler<T>`) where an earlier investigation (see git log / git blame on that doc, or just take my word for it) found the resolver only ever needs the type NAME for those, never the actual substituted value, so React's builtin generics were a non-issue. This bug is about USER-DEFINED generic type aliases where the alias BODY genuinely needs real substitution to produce correct output (e.g. `Omit<T, keyof U>` needs to know what `U`'s actual keys are to correctly omit them from `T`). Read that doc for full context before starting.

## What to investigate

1. **Read the current resolution code.** Start at `crates/core/src/resolver/chain.rs` (`resolve_props_chain`) and `crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs` — find exactly where/how a `CollectedType::Named { name, args }` reference to a user-defined type alias gets resolved, and confirm (don't assume) that type arguments are currently dropped/ignored rather than substituted. Also check `crates/core/src/extractor/alias.rs` and wherever `CollectedType::TypeAlias`/generic type parameter declarations get collected in the first place — do we even record that `Assign<T, U>` has type PARAMETERS (`T`, `U`) on its declaration, separately from any given call-site's type ARGUMENTS? If we don't track parameter names at all, that's the actual missing piece, not the substitution logic itself.

2. **Assess tractability.** Is this a simple, mechanical text/AST substitution problem (walk the alias body's AST/CollectedType tree, replace each `TypeReference` matching a parameter name with the corresponding argument's CollectedType, before running normal resolution on the result) — i.e., doable with plain structural substitution, no type inference needed? Or does it require deeper reasoning (e.g. conditional types depending on the substituted type, or recursive/self-referential aliases) that would need a real checker? Base your answer on the ACTUAL alias patterns real code uses — check what `Omit<T, keyof U> & U`-style aliases require: `keyof U` needs U's own resolved field names, which after substitution is just a normal resolved type (should be fine with structural substitution, no inference).

3. **Try a minimal implementation.** If it looks tractable, follow TDD (the project uses the superpowers:test-driven-development skill — write a failing test first in whatever module you end up modifying, matching existing test conventions e.g. in `crates/core/src/resolver/mod.rs`'s `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` or wherever appropriate for the change). Test something concrete like: a local generic type alias `type Assign<T, U> = Omit<T, keyof U> & U` used with concrete args, and confirm the resolved props reflect the substitution correctly (U's fields present, T's fields present minus the ones U overrides). Keep the implementation as small and structurally consistent with the existing resolver architecture as possible — this project has strict style rules (see `crates/core/CLAUDE.md` and the `rust-style` skill: no `unwrap()` outside `#[cfg(test)]`, `FxHashMap` for internal maps, exhaustive `match`, `let...else` for early returns, newtypes/enums over bare bools). Run `cargo test -p oxc-react-docgen-core` and `cargo clippy -p oxc-react-docgen-core --all-targets -- -D warnings` before considering it done.

4. If you get a clean, passing, clippy-clean fix: also update `fixtures/ark-ui/Select.tsx` and `fixtures/ark-ui/types.ts` back to closer-to-the-real `Assign<T,U>`-based upstream pattern (the fixture was deliberately rewritten away from this pattern earlier specifically because this bug blocked it) if doing so is a small, low-risk change — but only if you have time/confidence left; the core fix mattering more than restoring the fixture. Regenerate any snapshot tests if needed (`crates/core/tests/snapshots/`, or check for a snapshot/insta review step — look for a "snapshot" skill or `/snapshot` convention in this repo).

5. If it turns out genuinely hard (recursive substitution needed, conflicts with existing resolution architecture in a deep way, etc.), STOP and report why rather than forcing a bad fix.

## Report back (under 500 words)

- **Verdict:** tractable via structural substitution, or genuinely needs deeper work? Why?
- If you implemented a fix: what changed (files, approach), test results, clippy status, whether you also restored the fixture
- If you didn't: what you found, and a concrete recommendation for how a future implementation should approach it
- Any surprising discoveries about the existing type-alias/generic handling code

