This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   This session continued long-running work on `oxc-react-docgen` (a Rust-based, 10-100x faster drop-in replacement for `react-docgen-typescript`). In this most recent portion of the conversation, the user's explicit requests in order were:
   - "Shall we fix the issues you identified and mak[e] sure we're u[s]ing oxlint and oxfmt and making sure our MD is not wrapped ever?" — fix the docs/benchmarks.md-identified issues, ensure lint/format tooling is properly wired, enforce markdown never being wrapped.
   - "You're doing a lot with our CI workflow, are we following the best practices here? Are there better ways to do what we want to do? If so how? Why? When?" — a CI best-practices review question (answered inline in prose, no code changes requested).
   - "Do we have such a large gap to cover still? I thought we were way better???? WTF" — user alarm at a benchmark number, requiring a calm, data-backed clarification (answered: 72% of components are near-perfect matches, low aggregate is skewed by 9-10 outlier components needing a type checker).
   - "All our history should be using gabe@techworx.dev right?" — verification-only question about git author consistency.
   - "The benchmarks don't need to detail what was fixed during the report" — explicit instruction to remove the "process narrative" (bugs found/fixed while building the report) from docs/benchmarks.md, and just report current, correct state.
   - "Also, let's dig into the diff with RDT. Are those valid gaps or things we chose to not add to have the meaningful info vs overload??" — asked mid-turn while I was already editing the doc; required real data investigation, not a re-hash of old context.
   - "Don't we provide the same info in metadata/details/etc??" — a very specific follow-up asking whether the `ref`/`key` gap (the one deliberate omission found) is actually captured elsewhere in tool output (e.g., forwardRef metadata) rather than genuinely absent.
   - "Is there a way in the react-docgen format to document what we actually know? Is there a way for us to have a stop-gap solution to close that massive gap we have without horrendous performance? Maybe optionally?" — asked for (a) whether RDT's format has a channel for partial/degraded info, and (b) a low-cost, optionally-enabled fix for the type-checker-boundary gap without needing a real type checker (which would kill performance).
   - Via AskUserQuestion, user explicitly chose: "Default on (Recommended)" for whether the composes-population fix should be default behavior vs an opt-in flag, and "Implement now with TDD (Recommended)" for whether to implement immediately vs write a plan first.
   - Final message (this turn): "How does that change our benchmarks?" — with an explicit system-level instruction to respond with **TEXT ONLY, no tool calls**, since the user already has all needed context in the conversation.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - **`ComponentEntry.composes: Vec<String>`** — a field in `crates/core/src/types/output.rs` (line 63, with doc comment "Type names that could not be resolved (react-docgen compat)") that mirrors real `react-docgen`'s own documented `composes` field — its actual purpose: list a type by name/expression instead of flattening its (unresolvable) props into the props map.
   - **`ResolvedChain::empty_with_compose(type_name: String)`** — private helper in `crates/core/src/resolver/mod.rs` (~line 360): `Self { composes: vec![type_name], ..Default::default() }`. Already used at several existing call sites in `chain.rs` (known-opaque patterns, terminal React builtins, unresolvable imports) but NOT at the one dead end fixed this session.
   - **`ResolvedChain::merge_parent`** (`crates/core/src/resolver/mod.rs` ~line 365-393) — confirmed `self.composes.extend(parent.composes)` (line ~382) already correctly propagates `composes` through intersection/union member merges, meaning fixing the single leaf dead-end point was sufficient to bubble the info up to the top-level component.
   - **`resolve_base_as_chain`** (`crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs`, function starting ~line 122) — the actual dead-end function. Its final match arm (originally ~line 205-250) handles `CollectedType::Conditional`, `Mapped`, `IndexedAccess`, `Function`, `TemplateLiteral`, `TypeOf`, and primitive types used directly as a props "base" (e.g., an intersection member, or a Passthrough alias target) — these "cannot be used as a component's props base" per the diagnostic message it already built, but previously returned `ResolvedChain::default()` (fully empty, no trace).
   - **`serialize_rdt`** (`crates/cli/src/commands/extract.rs`, ~line 98) — builds the RDT-compatible JSON output; previously omitted `composes` entirely from its per-component JSON object (only `displayName`, `props`, `description`, `methods: []`, `tags`).
   - **`OpaqueReason` / `push_known_opaque_diagnostic`** (`crates/core/src/known.rs`) — the module that formats diagnostic messages like `"'{type_name}' is a conditional type and can't be statically resolved — it will appear as opaque"` (code `OpaqueType`) — confirmed this diagnostic ALREADY existed and was already firing for the outlier components, but its raw text was never also recorded into `composes`.
   - **TDD discipline** — every fix in this session followed RED (confirmed failing for the right reason) → GREEN → full test suite + clippy + fmt + snapshot check → commit, consistent with the project's established convention.
   - **`apps/validate`'s benchmarking harness** — `analyze.ts` computes per-prop agreement rate + type-diff taxonomy by comparing `NormalizedComponent.props` only; it does NOT read or compare `composes` at all, so the `composes` fix is orthogonal to and does not change any of the quantitative numbers in `docs/benchmarks.md`.
   - **`docs/benchmarks.md`** — the living report file, iteratively revised this session to (a) remove "process narrative" per explicit instruction, (b) add a precise 99.1%/0.9% breakdown of the RDT prop-gap, (c) add a 6th item documenting the `composes` stop-gap as a differentiator.
   - **`react_types::html_element_from_type_arg`** — new function added in the *previous* portion of this session (before this compaction's start) mapping concrete DOM element type names (e.g. `"HTMLDivElement"` → `"div"`, `"SVGSVGElement"` → `"svg"`) for the SVGAttributes/HTMLProps generic-element-mapping fix; mentioned here for continuity since it's referenced in `docs/benchmarks.md`'s "Shortcomings" section.
   - **The 10 outlier components** (from `docs/benchmarks.md`, unchanged by this session's `composes` work in terms of their `props` counts): `storybook-emotion/Button` (7 vs RDT 306), `fluentui/Button` (8 vs 303), `ariakit/menu-button/MenuButton` (5 vs 294), `base-ui/MenuTrigger` (11 vs 293), `ariakit/menu/Menu` (7 vs 286), `ariakit/menu-item/MenuItem` (5 vs 284), `ark-ui/Select/SelectRoot` (36 vs 314), `headlessui/Listbox/Listbox` (14 vs 246), `ListboxButton` (5 vs 234), `ListboxOption` (5 vs 234).

3. Files and Code Sections:
   - **`crates/core/src/resolver/alias.rs`** — the key fix location.
     - Read in full (447 lines) to trace the exact dead-end.
     - Modified the final match arm of `resolve_base_as_chain`:
       ```rust
       CollectedType::String
       | CollectedType::Number
       | CollectedType::Boolean
       | CollectedType::Null
       | CollectedType::Undefined
       | CollectedType::Any
       | CollectedType::Never
       | CollectedType::Unknown
       | CollectedType::Void
       | CollectedType::BigInt
