Determine: is there a common root cause across these three (e.g. "the codebase has no policy of deriving serialized/duplicated representations from a single source, so every hand-written mirror silently drifts as the source evolves"), and would a single class of fix apply to all three (e.g. schemars-derive for schema.rs, a shared precedence-check function called from both named.rs and chain.rs, a shared truncation-formatting helper for toon.rs)? Or are these three independent oversights that just happen to rhyme? Be honest if the fixes are genuinely unrelated even if the symptom (drift) rhymes.

Return your findings via the required schema.`,
  },
  {
    name: 'contract-and-cache-correctness',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

Your cluster: two smaller but distinct groups — (a) CLI exit-code/behavior contract inconsistency across commands, and (b) cache/state correctness bugs that produce wrong (not missing) output.

Findings assigned to this cluster (verify against the actual code): exit-code contract (0 success / 2 extraction-error) being documented and enforced only for extract/check, while watch always exits 0 regardless of extraction errors and inspect never surfaces Error-severity diagnostics found elsewhere in the tree (cli/main.rs:181-198 and the individual command files); DiagnosticCode::Unknown being defined but never constructed anywhere (types/diagnostic.rs:61); cache.rs's mtime+size cache key producing a stale hit on same-tick, same-size edits (cache.rs:181-191); ParsedProp allowing the contradictory state required:true + default_value:Some(_) with nothing preventing it (types/output.rs:79-99).

